---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<Base
  title="Accessibility statement"
  description="The accessibility state of this site, what was tested, and how to report a problem."
>
  <div class="wrap" style="padding-block: 2.5rem; max-width: 47rem;">
    <h1>Accessibility statement</h1>

    <div class="post-body">
      <p>
        {site.name} aims to be usable by everyone, including people with disabilities. The site is
        built to WCAG 2.1 level AA.
      </p>

      <h2>What the accessibility menu offers</h2>
      <p>
        The button in the corner of the screen opens a menu with six controls: text size up and
        down across four steps, high contrast, underlined links, wider line and letter spacing, and
        stopping animations and transitions. Your choice is stored in the browser and carries to
        the next page.
      </p>

      <h2>What is done beyond the menu</h2>
      <p>
        The whole site can be navigated by keyboard alone, every page starts with a skip link to
        the main content, and focus is clearly marked. Headings follow a real hierarchy, decorative
        illustrations are hidden from screen readers, and form fields are labelled. Code blocks are
        selectable text, not images.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site honours the operating system's reduce-motion setting without touching the menu. It
        has been checked with keyboard-only navigation and at 200% zoom.
      </p>

      <h2>Known limitations</h2>
      <p>
        Posts carry a lot of identifiers — repository names, commands, flags — that a screen reader
        pronounces as words. Command examples are shown as written, so punctuation-heavy lines can
        be tedious to hear.
      </p>
      <p>
        Posts are drafted with a language model. They are reviewed before publishing, but phrasing
        quality can vary between posts.
      </p>

      <h2>Reporting an accessibility problem</h2>
      <p>
        If you find a page or a component that is not accessible, we want to know. Get in touch
        through the <a href="/about">about page</a> and include the page address, what you were
        trying to do, and the assistive technology you were using.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
